    "workload": "Each timed batch is a current-step query; sequential batches await full completion; GPU uploads new rays, dispatches, copies output, awaits mapAsync, copies results into CPU-owned memory, then unmaps",
    "timing": "End-to-end wall latency per batch. CPU repetition count is calibrated per backend and case by doubling until >=4 ms total, capped at 1,048,576 repeats (calibration performed twice to warm JIT); same count used throughout a case and reported below. CPU totals divided by repeats; GPU always timed individually including readback. P95 across 15 samples (maximum order statistic). Browser file URL is not cross-origin isolated; GPU samples are timer-quantized. No GPU timestamp or CPU energy claim.",
    "algorithms": "Production Babylon finite-ray picking; custom CPU and WebGPU Moller-Trumbore triangle queries with either one whole-mesh AABB followed by a linear scan (brute force) or a median-split BVH (8 triangles per leaf). CPU and GPU prototypes share identical packed geometry, ray data, tolerances and traversal order.",
    "validation": "All tested rays match production Babylon hit/miss and distance within 3 mm; normals agree up to orientation (absolute dot >= 0.999). GPU matches CPU BVH with the same checks. Not an exhaustive robustness proof."
  },
  "limitations": "Synthetic benchmark in an idle browser, no renderer or streaming workload. Prototype implementations are experimental query kernels, not integrated collision backends; production query includes transforms/geodetic metadata omitted by the prototypes. CPU builds the BVH once; GPU copies it, with build/upload/compile measured separately. Float32 local coordinates required. Brute GPU uses one invocation per ray, not a parallel triangle reduction. Does not move JSBSim's CPU WASM contact solver to GPU. No game FPS, battery, watts, or cross-device claims. Repeated CPU batches are hot-cache throughput divided by repetitions; compare sub-timer-resolution values cautiously.",
